<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_alter_elabel.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-alterelabel">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-alterelabel">
  <primary>ALTER ELABEL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>ALTER ELABEL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>ALTER ELABEL</refname>
  <refpurpose>change an edge label</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
ALTER ELABEL [ IF EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">action</replaceable> [, ...]
ALTER ELABEL [ IF EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> RENAME TO <replaceable class="parameter">new_label_name</replaceable>

<phrase>where <replaceable class="parameter">action</replaceable> is one of:</phrase>

    ADD [ COLUMN ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable>
    DROP [ COLUMN ] <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> [ RESTRICT | CASCADE ]
    DISABLE INDEX
    SET STORAGE { PLAIN | EXTERNAL | EXTENDED | MAIN }
    SET TABLESPACE <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable>
    SET { LOGGED | UNLOGGED }
    CLUSTER ON <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable>
    SET WITHOUT CLUSTER
    INHERIT <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable>
    NO INHERIT <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable>
    REPLICA IDENTITY { DEFAULT | USING INDEX <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable> | FULL | NOTHING }
    OWNER TO <replaceable class="parameter">role_specification</replaceable>

<phrase>and <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable> is one of:</phrase>

    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED
    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED ( <replaceable class="parameter">property_key</replaceable> )
    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED ALWAYS AS ( <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> ) [ STORED ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-alterelabel-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>ALTER ELABEL</command> renames an edge label, changes its storage, attaches or
   detaches a parent, turns its indexes off, or adds and removes promoted typed columns. The
   label is looked up in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>, and the name cannot
   carry the graph. A vertex label is refused; the statement for one is
   <xref linkend="cypher-altervlabel"/>, which accepts every action listed here. The two differ
   only in what each kind of label gives an action to act on. An edge label has three indexes for
   <literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal> to invalidate and for <literal>CLUSTER ON</literal> to choose
   among, two more structural columns, and an edge label for <literal>INHERIT</literal> to take.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>RENAME TO</literal> is its own form. It cannot be combined with any other action, and
   a list of several actions cannot contain it. Several actions in one statement are separated by
   commas and applied in order. The base label <literal>ag_edge</literal> cannot be altered at
   all, and cannot be attached or detached as a parent.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>ADD COLUMN</literal> promotes a property to a real typed column beside the property
   map and backfills it from the rows already there, so the column is complete the moment the
   statement returns, and the statement fails on the first existing value that does not fit. The
   three forms and their restrictions are the ones <xref linkend="cypher-createelabel"/> sets
   out. The property key comes from the generation expression rather than from the column name,
   and is recorded in <structname>ag_label_property</structname>, so a query keeps naming the
   property. An edge weight a traversal filters or orders on is the usual candidate.
  </para>

  <para>
   Reshaping the label's table with <command>ALTER TABLE</command> instead is refused unless
   <varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> is on, and so are renaming one of its columns, moving it
   to another schema, and a SQL <command>MERGE</command> that writes it. Changing the type of
   <structfield>id</structfield>, <structfield>properties</structfield>,
   <structfield>start</structfield> or <structfield>end</structfield> stays refused even with the
   setting on. See <xref linkend="agensgraph-config"/>. The actions here never need it, because
   they update the graph catalog themselves.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-alterelabel-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Issue a notice instead of an error when the label does not exist.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Label to alter, in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>RENAME TO</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">new_label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Give the label a new name. Edges keep their ids, so <function>label()</function> reports the
      new name for rows created before the rename, and the sequence and indexes keep theirs.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ADD [ COLUMN ]</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Add a promoted typed column and backfill it. A property already promoted on the label is
      refused, and so is promoting the same key twice in one statement.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>DROP [ COLUMN ]</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <literal>[ RESTRICT | CASCADE ]</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Drop a column and, for a promoted property, forget its mapping, so reads of that key go
      back to the property map, which still holds the value. Indexes on the column go with it,
      and a structural column is refused. <literal>RESTRICT</literal>, the default, refuses while
      another object depends on the column; <literal>CASCADE</literal> drops those too.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Mark all three indexes on the label invalid, so that a bulk load does not maintain them.
      Reads and writes keep working, and the planner ignores an invalid index. There is no
      <literal>ENABLE INDEX</literal>; the way back is <xref linkend="cypher-reindex"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>SET STORAGE { PLAIN | EXTERNAL | EXTENDED | MAIN }</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Set the storage mode of the label's <structfield>properties</structfield> column, which
      decides whether a large property map is held inline or in the table's TOAST relation, and
      whether it is compressed. A label starts with <literal>EXTENDED</literal>. The mode applies
      to future writes and rewrites nothing stored already.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>SET TABLESPACE</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Move the label's table to another tablespace. Its indexes stay where they are.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>SET { LOGGED | UNLOGGED }</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Turn write ahead logging for the label's table on or off. Its id sequence follows.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CLUSTER ON</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Record an index for a later <command>CLUSTER</command> to use; it reorders nothing now.
      <literal>SET WITHOUT CLUSTER</literal> forgets that record.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>INHERIT</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Add the label as a child of another edge label in the same graph, so a pattern on the
      parent starts matching this label's rows. <literal>NO INHERIT</literal> removes it again.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>REPLICA IDENTITY { DEFAULT | USING INDEX <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable> | FULL | NOTHING }</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Choose which old values a write logs to identify a row, for logical replication.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>OWNER TO</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">role_specification</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Give the label to another role: a role name, <literal>CURRENT_ROLE</literal>,
      <literal>CURRENT_USER</literal> or <literal>SESSION_USER</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-alterelabel-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>, but build a
   graph of their own and drop it at the end. A rename carries the label's edges with it:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH alteredemo;
SET graph_path = alteredemo;
CREATE VLABEL city;
CREATE ELABEL road;
CREATE (:city {name: 'Seoul'})-[:road {km: 325}]-&gt;(:city {name: 'Busan'});
ALTER ELABEL road RENAME TO route;
MATCH ()-[e:route]-&gt;() RETURN count(e) AS routes;

 routes
--------
 1
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   <literal>ALTER ELABEL route DISABLE INDEX</literal> then marks all three indexes an edge
   label is created with invalid, and <literal>REINDEX ELABEL route</literal> builds them again.
   Promoting an edge property backfills the edges already there, and the read is then answered
   from the column, so it arrives as the column's type rather than as <type>jsonb</type>:
<programlisting>
ALTER ELABEL route ADD COLUMN km int GENERATED;
MATCH ()-[e:route]-&gt;() RETURN e.km AS km;

 km
-----
 325
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   <structname>ag_label_property</structname> maps a key to the column that answers for it. The
   two names differ whenever the explicit key form was used, and Cypher reads the key:
<programlisting>
ALTER ELABEL route ADD COLUMN carrier text GENERATED (operatedBy);
SELECT p.propname, a.attname FROM pg_catalog.ag_label_property p
  JOIN pg_catalog.ag_label l ON l.oid = p.laboid
  JOIN pg_attribute a ON a.attrelid = l.relid AND a.attnum = p.attnum
 WHERE l.relid = 'alteredemo.route'::regclass ORDER BY p.attnum;

  propname  | attname
------------+---------
 km         | km
 operatedby | carrier
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
   <literal>ALTER ELABEL route DROP COLUMN carrier</literal> takes the mapping with the column,
   and reads of <literal>operatedby</literal> go back to the property map. An endpoint column,
   by contrast, cannot be dropped at all:
<programlisting>
ALTER ELABEL route DROP COLUMN start;

ERROR:  cannot drop column "start" of graph label "route"
HINT:  It is a structural column of the graph label.
</programlisting>
   and neither can the table be reshaped with SQL while <varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> is
   off, which <literal>ALTER TABLE alteredemo.route ADD COLUMN lanes int</literal> reports as
   <literal>cannot add a column to graph label "route" with ALTER TABLE</literal>.
   <literal>DROP GRAPH alteredemo CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-alterelabel-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   A rename does not rename the label's sequence or its indexes, so after
   <literal>ALTER ELABEL road RENAME TO route</literal> the endpoint indexes are still
   <literal>road_start_idx</literal> and <literal>road_end_idx</literal>. Look a name up first.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>SET STORAGE</literal> takes four keywords, not five. <literal>DEFAULT</literal> is
   reserved and is a syntax error here; write <literal>SET STORAGE extended</literal> to go back
   to the mode a label starts with. The <literal>IF EXISTS</literal> notice differs between the
   two forms: <literal>label "nosuch" does not exist, skipping</literal> from
   <literal>RENAME TO</literal>, and <literal>graph label "nosuch" does not exist, skipping</literal>
   from an action list.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-alterelabel-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no statement that alters a label, because it has no labels. An edge type
   belongs to a graph type, created and dropped whole in clause 12.6, and nothing there matches
   renaming a label, promoting a property, or turning an index off.
  </para>

  <para>
   Every action here is an <command>ALTER TABLE</command> action and means what it means there,
   with three differences. <literal>SET STORAGE</literal> names no column and applies the mode to
   <structfield>properties</structfield>. <literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal> has no counterpart at
   all: it marks the label's indexes invalid, which SQL can otherwise only do by interrupting
   <command>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</command>. And <literal>ADD COLUMN</literal> takes only the
   promoted forms, where <command>ALTER TABLE</command> takes any column definition.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

